1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:04,783
On 7 April, Django 4.2
reached end of life.

2
00:00:04,817 --> 00:00:06,350
No more security fixes.

3
00:00:07,983 --> 00:00:12,317
But Debian 13 and Ubuntu
24.04 still ship it.

4
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:16,250
Since then, Django has published
19 security advisories…

5
00:00:16,283 --> 00:00:19,133
and not one official patch for 4.2.

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,317
On October 1, Ubuntu shipped
a backport for one of them.

7
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:28,500
Five days later, Django said
its original fix was incomplete.

8
00:00:28,533 --> 00:00:30,300
We took Ubuntu's source package,

9
00:00:30,333 --> 00:00:33,850
ran a test calibrated on
Django's own fix commits…

10
00:00:34,350 --> 00:00:38,417
and the code path Django closed is
still reachable in Ubuntu's build.

11
00:00:39,783 --> 00:00:42,017
That test was run by Heirloom.

12
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:44,583
An agent that keeps
end-of-life software patched…

13
00:00:44,617 --> 00:00:48,017
and proves which old versions
are actually vulnerable.

14
00:00:49,283 --> 00:00:53,333
Its first rule: never trust
a test that hasn't failed.

15
00:00:53,367 --> 00:00:57,050
For each advisory,
Heirloom writes its own test,

16
00:00:57,083 --> 00:01:01,417
proves it fails on Django's
vulnerable code and passes on the fix…

17
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:05,950
and only then asks the real question:
is the old version affected?

18
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:09,367
Then we let it run the whole campaign.

19
00:01:09,883 --> 00:01:13,917
19 advisories, replayed in the
order Django published them.

20
00:01:13,950 --> 00:01:15,850
13 candidate backports,

21
00:01:15,883 --> 00:01:20,433
each proven by a test that fails
before the fix and passes after.

22
00:01:20,467 --> 00:01:24,650
Where it couldn't prove something,
it filed the evidence instead of guessing.

23
00:01:24,683 --> 00:01:28,033
And when a new fix amended
one it had already backported,

24
00:01:28,067 --> 00:01:30,933
it stacked the follow-up
on its own pull request.

25
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:35,783
Across the whole campaign,
it asked me one question.

26
00:01:35,817 --> 00:01:39,250
A real judgement call,
about changing Django's behaviour.

27
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:41,200
I answered once.

28
00:01:41,233 --> 00:01:43,383
It sealed the answer as a precedent…

29
00:01:43,417 --> 00:01:46,433
and the next case reused it,
without asking.

30
00:01:47,667 --> 00:01:50,633
We tested the method on
cases with known answers.

31
00:01:50,667 --> 00:01:54,383
A naive pipeline once called
a vulnerable version safe.

32
00:01:54,417 --> 00:01:55,133
Heirloom?

33
00:01:55,167 --> 00:01:56,650
Zero times.

34
00:01:56,683 --> 00:02:00,517
Every claim in every pull request
links to its neatlogs trace,

35
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:04,883
and every backport links the Entire
session of the agent that wrote it.

36
00:02:05,983 --> 00:02:09,417
Each advisory cost about
20 cents of model time.

37
00:02:09,450 --> 00:02:12,467
We modelled the business in cfo.ai:

38
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:15,867
what that's worth to the teams
who keep old software alive.

39
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:18,083
Heirloom.

40
00:02:18,117 --> 00:02:20,483
Old software, kept honest.

41
00:02:20,517 --> 00:02:22,917
Every verdict is a test run you can open.
